Program fidelity has now split cleanly into two findings: the trusted translator reproduces `solution.mpy` byte-for-byte, and the submitted Python agrees with the canonical function on all 692 exhaustive lowercase strings of length ≤2 plus 194 generated lowercase cases. However, it diverges on every one of 191 generated mixed-character cases (for example, `"A"` is preserved by the canonical function but becomes `"y"`), and it raises `RecursionError` on a tested 1,100-character lowercase input that the canonical loop handles. I’m continuing because the proof can still be internally sound for the submitted program even when its intent coverage is defective.
